<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Celebration · c. 1930 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Rasa Sayang</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ms">Rasa Sayang (Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei)</bdi> / <bdi lang="ms-Latn">Rasa Sayange (Indonesia)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cheerful folk song sung across Malaysia, Indonesia, Singapore and Brunei. Its name means &quot;loving feeling&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Malay-speaking peoples of the Malay Archipelago (Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore); in Indonesia, the Ambonese of Maluku</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Ambonese Malay</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Malay Archipelago (Maluku, Indonesia, in the Indonesian account)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional; known and sung in public by the 1930s</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional folk song)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a>&quot;, literally &quot;loving feeling&quot;, is a folk song of the Malay Archipelago, popular in Brunei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. In Indonesia it is sung as &quot;Rasa Sayange&quot;. Its verses are pantun, the traditional Malay verse form: four lines rhyming ABAB with equal syllable counts, in which the first two lines set up the rhyme and the last two carry the message, often unrelated to the first pair. Only the opening verse breaks the regular syllable length.</p><p>In Brunei, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> it is known as a Malay folk song, also called &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a> Hey&quot;, related in form to Dondang Sayang and other pantun-based songs. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> it is known as an Ambonese Malay song from Maluku: the &quot;-e&quot; ending of &quot;Sayange&quot; is a feature of Moluccan Malay, heard also in &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a> Manise&quot; and &quot;Nona Manise&quot;, which the Malaysian version replaces with &quot;hey&quot;.</p><p>Its early history is known from films, broadcasts and press reports. A British documentary of 1938, Five Faces, used it as background music, and it was played on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> radio in 1939. In 1958 an unnamed singer in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> told The Straits Times of having first sung it before an audience more than twenty years earlier. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it appeared in the Japanese film Marai no Tora (1943), about a Japanese secret agent in Malaya. Usmar Ismail used it in his Indonesian films Darah dan Doa (1950) and Lewat Djam Malam (1954). In 1958 the World Assembly of Youth conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> adopted it as its theme tune, and in 1959 Malay students from Malaya sang it in Sydney at a fundraising exhibition for UNICEF. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a> Keris produced the Malay comedy film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a> Eh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1959; in 1960 George de Fretes recorded it on Indonesian Classics No. 1, and a Hindi version, sung by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar">Lata Mangeshkar</a> and Mohammed Rafi, was used in the film Singapore. The Lokananta record company gave it, on a souvenir LP of Indonesian folk songs, to the participants of the 4th Asian Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> in 1962. In 1989 the Singaporean singer-songwriter Dick Lee recorded a version with descriptions of Singaporean culture.</p><p>The question of whose song it is came to a head in 2007, when the Malaysian Tourism Board used it in a commercial for its &quot;Truly Asia&quot; campaign. Some Indonesians accused Malaysia of taking Indonesian heritage; Indonesian news sites reported it as a song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> that had appeared in early Indonesian films and recordings. In November 2007 around a thousand people demonstrated outside the Malaysian embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> against the use of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a>&quot; and other cultural items, such as Reog Ponorogo, in such advertisements, and the Indonesian government began an inventory of songs as the country's cultural property. Malaysia answered that the song is sung throughout the archipelago and belongs to its peoples, Malaysians and Indonesians alike: Tourism Minister Adnan Mansor called it a folk song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusantara">Nusantara</a>, of which Malaysia is part, and Rais Yatim, Minister of Culture, Arts and Heritage, recognised it as shared between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and Malaysia.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light, affectionate song of feeling for someone dear, built from pantun verses: each opens with an image from nature or daily life that sets the rhyme and ends with a message to the listener. The Malaysian and Indonesian versions differ in some verses and in the refrain's ending. The mood is warm and playful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Malay</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Malay</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Rasa sayang hey!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I feel love, hey!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Rasa sayang sayang hey!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I feel love, love, hey!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Hey lihat nona jauh,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hey, see that girl far off,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Rasa sayang sayang hey!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I feel love, love, hey!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Buah cempedak di luar pagar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The cempedak fruit is outside the fence,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Ambil galah tolong jolokkan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>take a pole and please knock it down;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Saya budak baru belajar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am a child just learning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Kalau salah tolong tunjukkan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if I am wrong please show me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Pulau pandan jauh ke tengah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pandan Island lies far out to sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Gunung Daik bercabang tiga,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mount Daik has three peaks;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Hancur badan dikandung tanah,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though the body crumbles in the earth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Budi yang baik dikenang juga,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a good deed is still remembered.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Dua tiga kucing berlari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Two, three cats are running;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Mana sama si kucing belang,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>none is like the striped cat.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Dua tiga boleh kucari,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Two, three I could find;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Mana sama abang seorang,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>none is like my one beloved.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Pisang emas dibawa berlayar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Golden bananas taken on a voyage,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Masak sebiji di atas peti,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>one ripens on top of a chest;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Hutang emas boleh dibayar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a debt of gold can be repaid,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ms">Hutang budi dibawa mati,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a debt of kindness is carried to the grave.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional: the chorus and four pantun, as quoted in the Malay Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://ms.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasa_Sayang" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>The verses are pantun, the Malay quatrain: the first two lines give an image, the last two the message, tied to the image by rhyme rather than sense. Any pantun can be sung to the tune between the choruses.</li><li>Stanza 4: abang (here &quot;my one beloved&quot;) is literally &quot;elder brother&quot;, the usual word for a sweetheart in Malay songs.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
